do $seed$
declare cid uuid;
begin
  select id into cid from public.courses where slug = 'bib-102';
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 1, $j$1.1 The Four Gospels and the Birth of Jesus$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "Matthew 1 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Matthew/1"}, {"title": "Luke 1 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Luke/1"}, {"title": "Luke 2 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Luke/2"}, {"title": "John 1 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/John/1"}], "body": "Read Matthew 1, Luke 1, Luke 2 and John 1 in the King James Version. Then read this lesson.\n\nThe New Testament opens with four Gospels: Matthew, Mark, Luke and John. The word gospel means good news. Each Gospel tells the story of Jesus Christ from a different point of view. Matthew writes to show that Jesus is the promised King and Messiah. Mark tells the story quickly and shows Jesus as the servant who acts. Luke, a physician, gives careful detail and shows Jesus as the Savior for all people. John shows Jesus as the eternal Word who is God. Matthew and Luke tell of His birth in Bethlehem to Mary, a virgin, and the angel told Joseph to call His name Jesus, because He would save His people from their sins.\n\nKey words: gospel, Messiah, virgin, Bethlehem, Savior.\n\nYour task: (1) Name the four Gospels and tell what the word gospel means. (2) Explain why the angel said to call Him Jesus (Matthew 1:21). (3) Tell what the angel told the shepherds in Luke 2:10 and 11.\n\nCourse area: Bible elective. Scripture is read from the King James Version.", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "Bible elective", "script": "Welcome to BIB 102, New Testament Survey. In the Old Testament we followed a promise from Genesis to Malachi. Now we open the New Testament and see that promise fulfilled in a person, Jesus Christ.\n\nThe New Testament begins with four books called the Gospels. The word gospel means good news. Matthew, Mark, Luke and John do not give four different stories. They give four views of the same Savior, the way four people standing at different places might describe the same mountain.\n\nMatthew was written with Jewish readers in mind. It begins with the family line of Jesus to show that He is the son of David and the son of Abraham, the promised King. Mark is the shortest and fastest Gospel, and it shows Jesus as the servant who is always on the move. Luke was written by a physician who investigated everything carefully, and he shows that Jesus came for all people. John shows us who Jesus really is, and its first verse says, In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God. In John 1:14 we read, And the Word was made flesh, and dwelt among us.\n\nMatthew and Luke both tell how Jesus was born. An angel came to Mary, a young woman in Nazareth, and told her she would bear a son. Joseph was troubled, but the angel said to him in Matthew 1:21, thou shalt call his name JESUS: for he shall save his people from their sins. The name Jesus means the LORD saves.\n\nJesus was born in Bethlehem, just as Micah had promised. Shepherds were watching their flocks that night, and the angel told them in Luke 2:10 and 11, Fear not: for, behold, I bring you good tidings of great joy, which shall be to all people. For unto you is born this day in the city of David a Saviour, which is Christ the Lord.\n\nYour task is to name the four Gospels and tell what gospel means, explain why the angel said to call Him Jesus, and tell what the angel said to the shepherds. Then take the four question check.\n\nThe good news began in a manger and it is for all people. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$1.1 The Four Gospels and the Birth of Jesus$j$);
end
$seed$;
